  <qti-item-body>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="show_interaction" outcome-identifier="INTERACTION_VISIBILITY" show-hide="show" class="qti-margin-0">
      <qti-content-body>
        <p>Which sentence uses &quot;extol&quot; correctly?</p>
    <qti-choice-interaction response-identifier="RESPONSE" shuffle="true" max-choices="1">
      <qti-simple-choice identifier="A">At the ribbon cutting, Councilwoman Ruiz extolled the crews who dug the tunnel, crediting a winter of cold, careful work.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="B">Asked about the quarterly report, the director extolled it with a shrug — &apos;fine, I guess&apos; — before moving to the next agenda item.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="C">The tenants extolled the landlord to fix the boiler at last, leaving three voicemails before Friday morning.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="D">The umpire extolled both dugouts for arguing balls and strikes, warning that the next outburst would empty the benches.</qti-simple-choice>
    </qti-choice-interaction>
      </qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>

    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-A" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Correct! Extol means to praise enthusiastically and publicly, so it&apos;s the correct answer because the councilwoman is lifting the crews up at a public ceremony, crediting exactly what they did well.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-B" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! Extol means to praise with enthusiasm — warmly and at some length, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because a shrug and &apos;fine, I guess&apos; is the faintest praise possible; extolling never mumbles.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-C" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! Extol means to praise, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because the tenants are urging the landlord to act — that&apos;s exhorting, a different ex- verb — and three voicemails about a broken boiler contain no praise at all.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-D" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! Extol means to praise, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because the umpire is reprimanding both dugouts — a warning about consequences is the opposite of talking someone up.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
  </qti-item-body>
